# Regression test for a real deadlock found while investigating why so many pty tests
# sleep after backgrounding a job before signaling it: `terminate`/`pause`/`resume`/
# `resume_foreground` used to notify every registered controller for a job *while still
# holding that job's own state lock* (`JobControlData`'s internal mutex). A
# `ChannelBasedController` sending into a bounded channel nobody is draining yet blocks
# -- and `cmd`'s own real controller (registered from inside its freshly-spawned worker
# thread, moments after `ThreadStore::spawn`'s generic one) uses exactly such a
# one-slot channel. `terminate` immediately followed by `fg`, with no sleep in between,
# could land `fg`'s own `resume_foreground` broadcast -- sent while still holding the
# job's lock -- into that already-full channel before `cmd`'s worker thread got far
# enough to drain it, permanently blocking the one thread that would ever drain it from
# ever reaching the point where it could (it needed that same lock, now held hostage,
# just to allocate its next command id). Confirmed directly via `sample`-based thread
# backtraces during this investigation.
#
# No sleep anywhere in this test, deliberately: the bug only reproduces when `terminate`
# and `fg` can land before `cmd`'s worker thread finishes its own setup, which a sleep
# would paper over. Looping gives the real race many chances to fire against genuine OS
# thread scheduling; if the underlying lock-holding bug is still present, this hangs and
# `run_system_test`'s own 30s timeout fails the test instead of ever reaching `echo`.
$i := 0
while {($i < 20)} {
    $job_id := $(cmd sleep 100 &)
    terminate $job_id
    try {
        $(fg $job_id)
    } catch {|$e|
    }
    $i = ($i + 1)
}
